========================================================================
D1  guard.py:591-592 -- is 'one file gets at most half the escalation
    budget' true?  Replay the exact expression at 591-597.
========================================================================
  no --budget  (total_deadline is None; file_share = escalate_budget/2)
  t (s into escalation)    clock left  file_share   ACTUAL slice = min(deadline, now+share)
  0                               600         300                                       300
  100                             500         300                                       300   <- NOT half of what is left
  299                             301         300                                       300   <- NOT half of what is left
  300                             300         300                                       300   <- NOT half of what is left
  301                             299         300                                       299   <- NOT half of what is left
  450                             150         300                                       150   <- NOT half of what is left
  599                               1         300                                         1   <- NOT half of what is left
  with --budget (total_deadline set; file_share = (deadline-now)/2)
  0                               600         300                                       300
  300                             300         150                                       150
  450                             150          75                                        75
  599                               1           0                                         0
  => the starvation cap is EXACTLY half only while >= escalate_budget/2
     of the clock remains.  After the halfway point the *next* file
     takes the entire remainder, which is the starvation the comment
     at 588-590 says it prevents.  --budget mode does not have this.

========================================================================
D2  SIGHT circumstantial thresholds -- guard.py:284, 287, 288.
    Which of them can change the law's ruling, and by how much?
========================================================================
  situation                                       byte  sight()
  nothing set (specificity 0.5, 300 lines)           0        6
  FAILONLY only (specificity >= 0.9)                 8        2
  SMALL only (0 < n_fail < 80)                      64        5
  NAMED only                                        16        3
  TOUCHED only (last 40 commits)                    32        4
  UBIQUITOUS only (specificity < 0.25)             128        7
  FAILONLY+NAMED+TOUCHED+SMALL                     120        2
  all circumstantial + UBIQUITOUS                  248        3
  FRAMED only (POINTING)                             1        0
  FRAMED + UBIQUITOUS                              129        0
  => every circumstantial threshold moves a file by at most one
     priority class, and none of them can beat a POINTING bit (R1).
     The 0.9 / 0.25 / 80 numbers therefore reorder files only WITHIN
     the circumstantial tier -- but that tier is the whole search
     order whenever no POINTING lane fired (guard.py:82-91's third
     refusal cause).

========================================================================
D3  RANK thresholds -- guard.py:403 (CHEAP < 8) and :412 (DENSE >= 8).
    rank.py:19 SPECIFIES '< 8' for CHEAP; rank.py:20 specifies NO
    number for DENSE.  Do they change the ruling?
========================================================================
  nothing                     byte=   0  rank()=7
  CHEAP only                  byte=  32  rank()=5
  DENSE only                  byte=  64  rank()=6
  SIGNALED                    byte=   8  rank()=3
  SIGNALED+CHEAP              byte=  40  rank()=3
  SIGNALED+DENSE              byte=  72  rank()=3
  FRAME+DENSE                 byte=  65  rank()=0
  FRAME+RETRIED (veto)        byte= 129  rank()=7
  => DENSE alone never changes a rank in these situations, but it is
     an input the law reads; its threshold (>= 8) exists ONLY in
     guard.py:412 and is pinned by no spec line and no test.
  => the '[:2]' at guard.py:402 is a THIRD hidden constant: CHEAP is
     computed from the first two kinds only, so a line whose third
     kind explodes the candidate set still measures as CHEAP.
